<ResearchGuideLayout
  title="How to Read a Certificate of Analysis (COA)"
  description="Learn how to review a Certificate of Analysis for product identity, lot information, report dates, methods, measured results, and laboratory documentation."
  eyebrow="COA GUIDE"
  heading="How to read a Certificate of Analysis."
  introduction="A Certificate of Analysis is a product-specific laboratory record. A useful review starts by matching the document to the item and lot in front of you before looking at any reported result."
>
  <p>A COA is most useful when it can be connected to a specific product record. The product name alone is not enough: identifiers, lot information, dates, and the laboratory report should work together as one traceable record.</p>

  <h2>Start with identity, not the result</h2>
  <p>Before reviewing a number or conclusion, confirm that the report describes the product and lot you intended to examine. A clear identity section reduces the chance of reading a valid report for the wrong item.</p>

  <ol class="rgv-guide-steps">
    <li><div><strong>Match the product name</strong><span>Compare the name or product identifier on the COA with the product page and physical label.</span></div></li>
    <li><div><strong>Confirm the lot or batch</strong><span>The lot number is the strongest link between a report and a particular production record.</span></div></li>
    <li><div><strong>Review report dates</strong><span>Look for received, tested, issued, or completed dates and understand what each date represents.</span></div></li>
    <li><div><strong>Identify the method</strong><span>A report should name the analytical method or test used to produce the stated result.</span></div></li>
    <li><div><strong>Read results with their units</strong><span>A value without its unit, basis, or stated method can be easy to misunderstand.</span></div></li>
  </ol>

  <h2>What the main COA fields mean</h2>
  <h3>Product name and identifier</h3>
  <p>This should be consistent with the associated catalog record. Some laboratories use an internal sample name in addition to the seller’s product identifier.</p>

  <h3>Lot or batch number</h3>
  <p>The lot distinguishes one documented batch from another. If the lot on the label and the lot on the report differ, the report should not be assumed to describe that item.</p>

  <h3>Analytical method</h3>
  <p>The method describes how the laboratory generated its measurement. Different methods answer different analytical questions, so the method belongs beside the result.</p>

  <h3>Measured result and units</h3>
  <p>Read the result exactly as reported, including units, qualifiers, and any stated limitations. A COA is a laboratory document; it does not determine suitability for a particular project by itself.</p>

  <div class="rgv-guide-callout">
    <strong>Fast verification rule</strong>
    <p>Product identifier + lot number + report source + method + result should form one consistent chain. If one part is missing or does not match, contact support before relying on the record.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Find a record in the RGVPRIME library</h2>
  <p>Use the <a href="/coa">RGVPRIME COA library</a> to search by product name, SKU, or the lot number printed on the product label. Available records can be reviewed before ordering and revisited later.</p>
</ResearchGuideLayout>
